			<div class="content" id="post-content-4848729">
									

<p>Hmmmm the problem I had before and the problem I have with a lot of first timers is that they end up giving a vague summary of/or a vague series of events and call it a narrative. While your updated pitch certainly is significantly improved, it still falls under very vague and not developed.</p>
<p>Maybe I can think of an example to help show you. Since you're doing something very biblical, I'll use God's creation of Adam and Eve as an example.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+I'll&nbsp;just&nbsp;add&nbsp;this&nbsp;example...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Yeet</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Imagine if the Garden of Eden and the creation of Adam and Eve and existence was someone's pitch. A lot of newbies would make something like this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Powerful being creates everything</li>
</ul>
<p>I'd ask for expansion and they'd do something along the lines of:</p>
<ul>
<li>Powerful being makes universe, makes a garden and puts life in garden</li>
</ul>
<p>When I want in-depth expansion. Not just a short summary replaced with a different, slightly more focused short vague summary.</p>
<p>What I want is:</p>
<ul>
<li>An omnipotent, powerful being takes seven days to create all forms of life and locates them in the Garden of Eden. He creates Adam and allows him to enjoy the garden. He then creates Eve to be his companion. One day, a snake tempts them with the fruit of knowledge and God casts them out of the Garden, starting off the human race as we know it.</li>
</ul>
<p>See what I mean? All three kinda mean the same thing (God creates everything) but the third one is the most interesting.</p>
<p>Your current one falls in a weird area slightly past the second example I have and way short of the third. I understand my example definitely isn't a perfect example, but I hope I helped elaborate on what I was looking for.</p>
<p>Now, I know this may disappoint, but I almost always only take two looks at any concept or draft. I'll be declining to return any more now. You can seek out other reviewers via the <a href="/butterfly-squad-roster">butterfly squad roster</a>. They're really good people, so don't be shy now. If any of them don't respond in a few days, you can always send followup PMs as long as you're not spamming them. Keep in mind if a reviewer is in high demand and has a busy personal life, there's a possibility they can gloss over you by mistake. They're not ignoring you, they're just busy people. Don't sweat it.</p>
<p>You're definitely getting there, so don't give up! The greenlight process can be a challenging one, but the end result is well, well worth it. Have a good day, partner.</p>
<p><strong>P.S.</strong> <span style="font-size:0.8em;">I'm not exactly a high demand reviewer, so thank you for reaching out to me and considering me 👍 it felt nice</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

